# Template for tools/bench/jdks.conf -- copy to jdks.conf and adjust.
#
#   cp tools/bench/jdks.conf.example tools/bench/jdks.conf
#
# Format, whitespace-separated:
#
#   label   jenv-version-name   branch
#
# The label appears in tables and in every filename. The jenv name is resolved with
# `jenv prefix` and verified again from each recording afterwards. The branch is the
# worktree the arm builds from (irrelevant when artifacts are pre-staged; see bench.conf).
# The runner requires AT LEAST TWO arms.
#
# --- traps recorded on this machine ------------------------------------------------
#
# USE FULLY-QUALIFIED JENV NAMES ONLY. Bare aliases go stale and can resolve to another
# vendor entirely: `jenv prefix 25.0.4` and `jenv prefix graalvm-25` are both GraalVM,
# which compares JIT compilers rather than JDK versions. Never put GraalVM in a
# JDK-version matrix. Aliases also lie about patch level -- `openjdk64-21.0.11` resolves
# to 21.0.12.1 here, which is fine but must be read from the recording, not the label.
#
# A `.java-version` file in the project root silently overrides `jenv global`, so every
# arm would run the same JVM under different labels. The runner moves it aside and
# restores it; verify it did.
#
# Two arms cannot check out the SAME branch through `git worktree add`. Pre-create them
# detached and the runner will reuse them:
#   git worktree add --detach /tmp/jdkcmp-<label> <branch>
#
# --- example: two questions at once, sharing an arm --------------------------------
#
# A pure-JVM comparison and an as-shipped comparison share their baseline, so three
# interleaved arms answer both for fewer runs than two separate matrices -- and 25 vs 25s
# additionally isolates the compile target with the JVM held fixed:
#
#   21    release-21 jar on JDK 21    <- baseline
#   25    the SAME jar on JDK 25      <- pure JVM effect      (21 vs 25)
#   25s   release-25 jar on JDK 25    <- upgrade as shipped   (21 vs 25s)
#
# Stage arms 21 and 25 with a byte-identical jar and verify with shasum, or their delta
# is not the runtime alone.
21   openjdk64-21.0.11   main
25   openjdk64-25.0.3    main
25s  openjdk64-25.0.3    java25

# --- other configurations worth keeping -------------------------------------------
#
# A/A noise floor -- two arms, same JDK, byte-identical jar. Any delta it reports is noise
# BY CONSTRUCTION, so it calibrates the study design itself rather than just run-to-run
# spread, and it would catch a systematic ordering or interleaving bias. Run this BEFORE
# trusting any small effect. On 2026-09-30 it returned +1.71% on arms that differ in
# nothing, with a 4.70% full spread -- larger than every 21-vs-25 throughput difference
# this project had reported.
#   21a  openjdk64-21.0.11   main
#   21b  openjdk64-21.0.11   main
#
# Single-variable code A/B -- same JDK, same commit, one line changed, artifacts staged
# per arm. This is how the 2026-09-30 executor result was measured.
#   fj   openjdk64-21.0.11   main
#   vt   openjdk64-21.0.11   main
